// 22491 real asteroids, from NASA/JPL's Small-Body Database (retrieved 2026-09-04):
//   https://ssd-api.jpl.nasa.gov/doc/sbdb_query.html
//
// Main-belt asteroids and Jupiter Trojans brighter than absolute magnitude 14 --
// roughly, larger than about 6 km -- with osculating elements at the common
// epoch below. Eight objects on other epochs were dropped rather than
// complicate the format, and the four largest (Ceres, Vesta, Pallas, Hygiea)
// are excluded here because they appear as real bodies instead; see data.js.
//
// These are the actual orbits, not a scattering of plausible-looking rocks, and
// that matters: the Kirkwood gaps cleared by resonance with Jupiter, and the two
// Trojan clouds sixty degrees ahead of and behind it, are in the render because
// they are in the solar system, not because anyone drew them in.
//
// Packed as seven uint16 per object -- a, e, inclination, longitude of
// ascending node, argument of perihelion, mean anomaly, absolute magnitude --
// each linearly quantised over the range below, then base64'd. That is 14 bytes
// per asteroid instead of 56. The coarsest quantum is 0.0055 degrees of angle,
// about 21,000 km at 3 AU, which is far below anything resolvable on an object
// drawn as a single speck.
//
// Trojans are separable from belt objects by semi-major axis alone: the belt
// ends near 3.2 AU and the Trojans sit at 4.9-5.4 AU, sharing Jupiter's orbit.

export const ASTEROID_EPOCH = 2461200.5;      // Julian date of the elements
export const ASTEROID_COUNT = 22491;
export const TROJAN_MIN_AU = 4.5;      // clean divide between the two families

